// Software shown in the Software card of the PC Details window, in this order (2 per row).
// Each program is checked in this order: builtIn -> files -> registry -> content -> uninstall.
// The first source that finds a version wins; otherwise it shows "Not installed".
//
//   name       - label shown in the window ("Version" is added automatically).
//                Also the key used to look up its minimum version in baselines.json.
//   label      - optional; shown exactly as written instead of "<name> Version".
//   process    - checks whether a process is running instead of finding a version, e.g. "nschill.exe".
//                Running = "Running / <users running it>" in green; not running = "NA / NA" in red.
//                baselines.json and the version sources below don't apply to process entries.
//   files      - exe/dll paths on the remote PC (C:\... is read through \\PC\c$); shows the file's version.
//   registry   - { "key": "SOFTWARE\\...", "value": "ValueName" } under HKLM; shows that value.
//   content    - { "path": "C:\\...\\version.txt", "pattern": "Version=([0-9.]+)" }
//                Reads the file and shows the text captured by ( ) in the pattern (a regular expression).
//   uninstall  - start of the name shown in Programs & Features (not case-sensitive); shows that entry's version.
//   builtIn    - special handlers that need custom code. Only "Office" is available.
//   required   - true = show "Not installed" in red. Otherwise it shows in normal text.
//
// JSON needs every backslash written twice (\\), including inside patterns.
//
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// Writing a "pattern" for content (regular expressions, .NET syntax)
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
// The pattern searches the file's text. Whatever is inside the first ( ) is shown as the version.
// If the pattern has no ( ), the whole match is shown. Only the first match in the file is used.
// Patterns are case-sensitive; start with (?i) to ignore case. ^ and $ mean start / end of a line.
//
//   In a pattern        Means                                       Write it in JSON as
//   .                   any single character                        .
//   \d                  a digit 0-9                                 \\d
//   \s                  a space or tab                              \\s
//   \.                  a real dot (a plain . means "any character") \\.
//   [0-9.]              one digit or dot                            [0-9.]
//   [^"]                any character except "                      [^\"]
//   +                   one or more of the previous item            +
//   *                   zero or more of the previous item           *
//   ?                   the previous item is optional               ?
//   ( )                 the part to show as the version             ( )
//   (?i)                ignore upper / lower case (put at the start) (?i)
//
// Examples (file contents -> pattern as written in this file -> result):
//   4.2.17                            "([0-9.]+)"                               4.2.17
//   Version=4.2.17                    "Version=([0-9.]+)"                       4.2.17
//   Version = 4.2.17                  "Version\\s*=\\s*([0-9.]+)"               4.2.17
//   version: 4.2.17  (any case)       "(?i)version:\\s*([0-9.]+)"               4.2.17
//   ProductVersion=12.5.0.311         "ProductVersion=(\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+\\.\\d+)"   12.5.0.311
//   <version>3.1.4</version>          "<version>([^<]+)</version>"              3.1.4
//   "version": "2.0.9"                "\"version\"\\s*:\\s*\"([^\"]+)\""       2.0.9
//   Build 1234 (Release)              "Build (\\d+)"                            1234
//   a line that is only a version     "^([0-9.]+)$"                             4.2.17
//
// If the pattern matches nothing, the value shows "Version not found" in orange (hover for details).
// Test a pattern at https://regex101.com (choose the .NET flavor) using single backslashes,
// then double every backslash when you paste it here.
// ---------------------------------------------------------------------------------------------
//
// Edit this file (Config\software.json next to HelpDeskProTools.exe); changes apply the next time PC Details is opened or refreshed.
{
  "software": [
    {
